+		<English>[TAB]A crossbow is a ranged weapon using an elastic launching device consisting of a bow-like assembly called a prod, mounted horizontally on a main frame called a tiller, which is hand-held in a similar fashion to the stock of a long gun. Crossbows shoot arrow-like projectiles called bolts or quarrels. A person who shoots crossbow is called a crossbowman, an arbalister or an arbalist (after the arbalest, a European crossbow variant used during the 12th century).[PARAGRAPH:1][TAB]On top of the usual info that can be found on the [LINK=UNIT_CROSSBOWMAN]Crossbowman's page[\LINK], the repeating crossbow, also known as the repeater crossbow, and the Zhuge crossbow, also romanized Chu-ko-nu, due to its association with the Three Kingdoms-era strategist Zhuge Liang (181-234 AD), is a crossbow invented during the Warring States period in China that combined the bow spanning, bolt placing, and shooting actions into one motion.[PARAGRAPH:1][TAB]The earliest archaeological evidence of the repeating crossbow is found in the state of Chu, but it uses a pistol grip that is different from the later and more commonly known Ming dynasty (1368-1644) design.[PARAGRAPH:1][TAB]Although the repeating crossbow was in use throughout most of Chinese history until the late Qing dynasty (1644-1911), it was generally regarded as a non-military weapon suited for women, defending households against robbers.</English>
